SPU: update event_status atomically (open upstream PR, imported as is).

spu_context.event_status is written by the SPU's own thread (list stall Sn, tag-group event, WrEventAck, its Lr poll)
and by other threads (PPU stores and peer SPU PUT/PUTLLC raising Lr through spu_coherency.c), each with a plain
read-modify-write, so one side's bit could be lost. Resistance 3 runs the same WWS job manager and Insomniac job
dispatcher as Resistance: Fall of Man, where a lost Sn hung the first gameplay frame; here the PPU rewrites the SPURS
line in bursts (cellSpursReadyCountStore) while the job manager parks lists. No hang of that kind was seen in
Resistance 3, and two scripted runs with the PR showed no change (Home level, stick turning, 26.4-26.6 fps; the
multi-second freeze 45 s into gameplay is still there): it is kept because the race is in code this game runs on
every frame, the PR's tests pass and it costs nothing measurable. Drop it if upstream rejects it.

Upstream: PR #492 (drop this file when the pin contains it).

---

diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_channels.c b/runtime/spu/spu_channels.c
index 59f5e4b8..cd4de7ff 100644
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_channels.c
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_channels.c
@@ -842,7 +842,7 @@ void spu_wrch(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t channel, u128 value)
          * long-missing producer for event_status (previously only ever cleared
          * via WrEventAck -> RdEventStat waits could never complete). */
         if (channel == MFC_WrTagUpdate && v != 0 && (ctx->event_mask & 0x1u)) {
-            ctx->event_status |= 0x1u;
+            SPU_EVENT_SET(ctx, 0x1u);
             spu_ch_wake(ctx);
         }
         return;
@@ -899,7 +899,15 @@ void spu_wrch(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t channel, u128 value)
          * it still holds a reservation it has already lost, which is the parked
          * SPURS kernel this whole mechanism exists to wake. */
         spu_lockline_lock();
-        ctx->event_status &= ~v;
+        SPU_EVENT_CLEAR(ctx, v);
+        /* A stall-and-notify nobody has read yet stays pending. We run a
+         * resumed list inline, inside the MFC_WrListStallAck that resumes it,
+         * so its next stall can land BEFORE the handler acknowledges the event
+         * it was taken for; on hardware the MFC gets there later, after the
+         * ack. Acking then cleared that new edge while the list stayed parked
+         * (list_stall_stat set, Sn clear). */
+        if ((v & 0x2u) && ctx->list_stall_stat)
+            SPU_EVENT_SET(ctx, 0x2u);
         spu_lockline_unlock();
         break;
     case SPU_WrSRR0:         ctx->srr0 = v;                                 break;
@@ -960,7 +968,7 @@ static void spu_resv_lost_poll(spu_context* ctx)
     if (!vm_base || ctx->resv_ea == 0) return;
     if (memcmp(vm_base + ctx->resv_ea, ctx->resv_line, 128) != 0) {
         ctx->resv_valid = 0;
-        ctx->event_status |= 0x400u;
+        SPU_EVENT_SET(ctx, 0x400u);
     }
 }
 
